.cpcache/
target/
node_modules/

# Build outputs. They were tracked, and that opened the exact hole that bit
# nexus-x402 on 2026-08-31: `wrangler deploy` ships `dist/worker/worker.js`,
# and the deploy guard checks the CHECKOUT's git state, not the artifact's
# freshness. So a checkout perfectly in step with main could ship a bundle
# that disagreed with its own source, and the guard stayed green. It happened:
# a stale build went out over a fix and put a wrong public number back.
#
# Untracking them means a deploy has to build first, which is what the
# resource-guard flow does anyway. It also removes a subtler trap -- a stale
# `dist/` is indistinguishable from a fresh one until you compare sizes, so a
# build that never ran (the lock was held, every retry refused) reads as a
# build that succeeded.
dist/
.shadow-cljs/
